      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960199999999986,
        622.19130000000018,
        72.970066709862408,
        11.955199999999991
      ],
      "text" : "Example 24."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        185.04019999999997,
        598.94300799999996,
        95.875034712832075,
        20.455347200000006
      ],
      "text" : "2+5· 3 Add First"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        320.03988000000004,
        598.94300799999996,
        90.061243648409572,
        20.455347200000006
      ],
      "text" : "2+ 5· 3 Multiply"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        194.39999999999998,
        583.94300799999996,
        204.38358483584011,
        20.455347200000006
      ],
      "text" : "7· 3 Multiply 2+ 15 Add"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        198.12000000000003,
        577.05944480000005,
        211.10490611801615,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "21 Solution 17 Solution"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        542.49924480000004,
        425.15760431178239,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "The previous example illustrates that if the same problem is done two diﬀerent"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        528.09924480000006,
        425.20286774781448,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "ways we will arrive at two diﬀerent solutions. However, only one method can be"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        513.69924480000009,
        425.25699774602242,
        11.979110399999968
      ],
      "text" : "correct. It turns out the second method, 17, is the correct method. The order of"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        499.29924480000005,
        425.17502307330562,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "operations ends with the most basic of operations, addition (or subtraction)."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        484.89924480000008,
        425.15609772416002,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "Before addition is completed we must do repeated addition or multiplication (or"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        470.4992448000001,
        425.18523314936317,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "division). Before multiplication is completed we must do repeated multiplication"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        456.09924480000012,
        425.15779774947839,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "or exponents. When we want to do something out of order and make it come first"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        441.69924480000014,
        425.25485616289279,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "we will put it in parenthesis (or grouping symbols). This list then is our order of"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        427.29924480000017,
        236.29307477370878,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "operations we will use to simplify expressions."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        238.20000000000005,
        408.33904480000018,
        118.70197760000002,
        11.967155199999979
      ],
      "text" : "Order of Operations:"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        228.36000000000001,
        384.95119999999997,
        138.42439704207356,
        11.955199999999991
      ],
      "text" : "Parenthesis (Grouping)"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        266.6397,
        365.99139999999994,
        61.948110758656014,
        11.955199999999991
      ],
      "text" : "Exponents"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        191.03969999999998,
        347.03119999999996,
        213.06491423146497,
        11.955199999999991
      ],
      "text" : "Multiply and Divide (Left to Right)"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        198.11977999999999,
        327.95149999999995,
        198.90507762966013,
        11.955199999999991
      ],
      "text" : "Add and Subtract (Left to Right)"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999995,
        308.97934479999998,
        425.18288441799677,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "Multiply and Divide are on the same level because they are the same operation"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999967,
        294.5793448,
        425.155722080256,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "(division is just multiplying by the reciprocal). This means they must be done left"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999967,
        280.17934480000002,
        425.20106227448321,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "to right, so some problems we will divide first, others we will multiply first. The"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999967,
        265.77934480000005,
        418.45389098572792,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "same is true for adding and subtracting (subtracting is just adding the opposite)."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999967,
        246.81914480000006,
        425.21833906938883,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "Often students use the word PEMDAS to remember the order of operations, as"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999967,
        232.41914480000008,
        425.20380585469439,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "the first letter of each operation creates the word PEMDAS. However, it is the"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        492.36027999999993,
        219.81914480000006,
        7.9621632000000204,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "P"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        492.36027999999993,
        205.41914480000008,
        7.9621632000000204,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "E"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.959779999999967,
        198.57934480000006,
        393.13323619422721,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "author’s suggestion to think about PEMDAS as a vertical word written as:"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        486.48019999999991,
        191.01914480000005,
        19.742524855808085,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "MD"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        488.76027999999991,
        176.61914480000007,
        15.26380423014399,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "AS"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960279999999898,
        161.01914480000005,
        425.20302815513605,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "so we don’t forget that multiplication and division are done left to right (same"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960279999999898,
        146.61914480000007,
        425.25878155092482,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "with addition and subtraction). Another way students remember the order of"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960279999999898,
        132.21914480000004,
        425.21829506073595,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "operations is to think of a phrase such as “Please Excuse My Dear Aunt Sally”"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960279999999898,
        117.81914480000005,
        424.69375233856005,
        11.97911040000001
      ],
      "text" : "where each word starts with the same letters as the order of operations start with."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960279999999898,
        98.859344800000045,
        425.20346330393613,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "World View Note: The first use of grouping symbols are found in 1646 in the"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960279999999898,
        84.459344800000039,
        425.15704791731207,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "Dutch mathematician, Franciscus van Schooten’s text, Vieta. He used a bar over"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 9.9626400000000004,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        299.63947999999993,
        52.379053200000037,
        10.021160447733621,
        9.8729762399999998
      ],
      "text" : "18"
    }
